::GEO_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is a conjecture? {
~A point is an exact location. #This is a definition, not a conjecture based on observations.
=I think these segments are congruent because they have matching tick marks. #Correct. This is a claim based on observed diagram evidence.
~The answer is correct because I chose it. #This is not a mathematical conjecture or valid support.
~A diagram is a drawing of geometric objects. #This is a description, not a conjecture to test or support.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student claims two angles are congruent. Which evidence supports the claim? {
~The angles are close together. #Location does not prove congruence. The evidence must relate to angle measure.
~The angles look about the same size. #Appearance alone is not enough support for a Geometry claim.
=The angles have matching arc marks. #Correct. Matching arc marks are relevant evidence that angles are congruent.
~The angles are drawn in dark pencil. #Drawing style does not support a mathematical conclusion.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which argument is flawed? {
~The segments are congruent because they have matching tick marks. #This is supported by relevant diagram evidence.
~The angle is a right angle because it has a square corner mark. #This is supported by the right angle marking.
~The points are collinear because they lie on the same straight line. #This is supported by the definition of collinear points.
=The lines are parallel because they look parallel. #Correct. Appearance alone is not enough evidence for a parallel-line conclusion.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What does a valid mathematical argument need? {
~A claim with no explanation #A claim alone is incomplete because it does not show why the conclusion is supported.
=A claim, relevant evidence, and reasoning that connects them #Correct. A valid argument explains why the evidence supports the claim.
~A drawing that looks accurate #A drawing can help, but appearance alone does not create a valid argument.
~A conclusion based on one guess #A guess is not enough support for a mathematical argument.
}

::GEO_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L03_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student says a conjecture is true because one example worked. What is the best correction? {
~One example proves every conjecture. #One example can suggest a conjecture, but it does not prove a general claim.
~A conjecture never needs evidence. #A conjecture should be tested and supported before it is accepted.
~The conjecture must be false. #One example is not enough to prove the conjecture false either.
=One example can suggest a conjecture, but more support is needed. #Correct. Stronger evidence or reasoning is needed before accepting a conjecture.
}
